==English==



===Etymology===
From {{Hebr.}} [[יִשְׁמָעֵאל]] (Ishmail), meaning: "God hears or obeys". Standard Yišmaʿel, Tiberian Yišmāʿêl; Arabic: {{ARchar|[[إسماعيل]]}}, (Ismā'īl).

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun|-}}

# {{context|Judaism|Islam}} The eldest [[son]] of [[Abraham]] and his wife's handmaiden [[Hagar]] who were cast out after the [[birth]] of [[Isaac]]; [[traditionally]] the [[ancestor]] of the [[Arab]]s.
# {{given name|male|}}.

====Quotations====
* '''1611''' — [[w:King James Version of the Bible|King James Version of the Bible]], ''[[s:Bible (King James)/Genesis|Genesis]] 16:15''
*: And Hagar bare Abram a son: and Abram called his son's name, which Hagar bare, '''Ishmael'''.
* '''1851''' — [[w:Herman Melville|Herman Melville]], ''[[s:Moby-Dick|Moby-Dick]]'', ch 1
*: Call me '''Ishmael'''.

